.TH sparsemap 7 "2026-06-03" "sparsemap 4.0.0" "Sparsemap Concepts"
.SH NAME
sparsemap \- conceptual overview of the compressed bitmap library
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sparsemap is a sparse, compressed bitmap library written in C.  It is
optimized for workloads that contain long runs of consecutive set or
unset bits.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
Three-tier hierarchy:
.TP
.B Tier 0 \(em bit vectors
Individual bits packed into 64-bit words
.RB ( __sm_bitvec_t ).
.TP
.B Tier 1 \(em chunks
Each chunk encodes up to 2048 bits.  Two encodings:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Sparse:
a 64-bit descriptor with 32 \(times 2-bit flags identifying which
sub-vectors are all-zero, all-one, mixed, or unused.  Only mixed
sub-vectors store an explicit 64-bit payload.  All-zero and all-one
sub-vectors take zero payload.
.IP \(bu 2
.B RLE:
a single 64-bit descriptor representing a run of N set bits
starting at index 0 of the chunk.  An RLE chunk takes 8 bytes
regardless of run length, up to about 2 billion bits.
.TP
.B Tier 2 \(em map
The top-level
.B sm_t
manages an ordered sequence of chunks plus a 4-byte chunk count.
The map grows and shrinks the underlying byte buffer as chunks are
added or removed.
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
PostgreSQL extensions tracking TID sets, bitmap heap scans,
posting lists.
.IP \(bu 2
Trigram or n-gram indexes where document identifiers cluster.
.IP \(bu 2
Allocation bitmaps for storage engines.
.IP \(bu 2
Anywhere you'd reach for CRoaring but want a smaller, simpler
library and don't need 32-bit integer support out of the box.
.SH WHEN NOT TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Multi-threaded workloads.  Sparsemap is not thread-safe.  A
lock-free / wait-free variant is in design (see the
.B experiment/thread-safe
branch).
.IP \(bu 2
32-bit integer universes.  Sparsemap indexes with 64-bit values.
.SH PORTABILITY
Scalar C99 only: no SIMD intrinsics, no target-specific code.  The
same source compiles unchanged on x86_64, aarch64, ARM, and RISC-V,
with both gcc and clang, and on Windows with MSVC, MinGW, or clang.
Continuous integration exercises x86_64, aarch64, riscv64, and a
big-endian (s390x) target.
.PP
The on-disk layout puts the 4-byte chunk count and chunk-start
offsets at 4-byte boundaries and the 8-byte chunk descriptors at
4-aligned addresses.  All such accesses go through
.IR memcpy -based
helpers or a 1-byte-aligned bitvector type, so the codec is safe on
strict-alignment cpus; on x86_64 and aarch64 the compiler lowers them
to single native loads with no overhead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sparsemap (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Gregory Burd <greg@burd.me>.
